.TH SU 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
su \- run a shell with a different user ID
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B su
.RB [ \-l | \-\-login | \- ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR command ]
.RI [ user ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B su
starts the shell of
.I user
(default
.BR root )
with that user's user ID, group ID and supplementary groups.
Arguments are scanned left to right; options and the
.I user
operand may appear in any order, and a later
.I user
operand replaces an earlier one.
.PP
If the invoking real user ID is not 0,
.B su
prompts for a password on standard input, with echo disabled when
standard input is a terminal, and checks it against the second field of
the user's line in
.I /etc/shadow
using
.BR crypt (3).
If the user has no
.I /etc/shadow
entry and the
.BR passwd (5)
password field does not begin with
.BR x ,
that field is used instead.
An empty stored password accepts any input; a stored password beginning
with
.B *
or
.B !\&
never matches.
The real user ID 0 skips the prompt entirely.
.PP
After authentication
.B su
calls
.BR setgid (2),
.BR initgroups (3)
and
.BR setuid (2),
sets
.B USER
and
.B LOGNAME
to the target user's name, and executes the target user's shell from
.BR passwd (5),
or
.I /bin/sh
if that field is empty.
All other environment variables are passed through unchanged.
.PP
.B su
must be installed set-user-ID root to switch users; the root file system
build installs
.I /bin/su
with mode 4755.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login ", " \-
Start a login shell: additionally set
.BR HOME ,
.BR SHELL ,
.B USER
and
.B LOGNAME
from the target user's
.BR passwd (5)
entry, change to the user's home directory (or to
.I /
if that fails), and execute the shell with the argument
.BR \-l .
If that
.BR exec
fails, the shell is retried with no arguments.
.TP
.BI \-c " command"
Execute the shell as
.IB shell " \-c " command
instead of starting an interactive shell.
.PP
Any other argument that begins with
.B \-
is rejected.
.SH EXIT STATUS
On success
.B su
does not return; the exit status is that of the executed shell.
It exits with status
.B 1
if an option is invalid, the user does not exist, authentication fails,
the user or group ID cannot be changed, or the shell cannot be executed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR USER ", " LOGNAME
Always set to the target user's name.
.TP
.BR HOME ", " SHELL
Set from the target user's entry only with
.BR \-l .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/passwd
User database, read via
.BR getpwnam (3).
.TP
.I /etc/shadow
Password hashes.
.SH EXAMPLES
Become root with a login environment:
.EX
$ su \-
.EE
.PP
Run one command as another user:
.EX
$ su \-c 'id' daemon
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR id (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR whoami (1),
.BR setgid (2),
.BR setuid (2),
.BR crypt (3),
.BR getpwnam (3),
.BR passwd (5),
.BR shadow (5)
.SH BUGS
.B su
does not log its use, does not sanitize the inherited environment, and
does not consult the target account's expiry fields in
.IR /etc/shadow .
Passwords longer than 127 bytes are truncated.
